    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/spectro/electronic/plot_01_franck_condon`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Indirect spin-spin coupling carried through the bonding electrons (Ramsey and Purcell, 1952) splits an NMR resonance into a multiplet. The splitting is J Hz regardless of field strength. In the first-order limit, n equivalent spin-1/2 neighbours give n+1 lines with Pascal&#x27;s-triangle intensities. This example simulates ethanol&#x27;s -CH3 triplet and -CH2- quartet, then a doublet of triplets from two inequivalent coupling partners with different J values.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/spectro/nmr/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_nmr_multiplets_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/spectro/nmr/plot_01_nmr_multiplets`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Spin-spin (J) coupling and the n+1 rule: ethanol's triplet/quartet and a doublet of triplets</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/spectro/nmr/plot_01_nmr_multiplets`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Karplus (1959) used valence-bond theory to show that the three-bond (vicinal) H-C-C-H coupling constant depends on the dihedral angle \phi. It is large for eclipsed (0 degrees) and anti (180 degrees) protons and nearly zero near 90 degrees. This lets a measured ^3J report molecular conformation. This example plots Karplus&#x27;s 1959 curve from karplus_coupling, reads off the axial-axial and axial-equatorial couplings of a cyclohexane chair, and shows the resulting doublet splittings.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/spectro/nmr/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_03_karplus_vicinal_coupling_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/spectro/nmr/plot_03_karplus_vicinal_coupling`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">The Karplus relation: vicinal 3J(H,H) coupling as a function of dihedral angle</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/spectro/nmr/plot_03_karplus_vicinal_coupling`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Dennison (1926) gave the quantum-mechanical rotational energy levels E_J=\hbar^2J(J+1)/(2I). With the \Delta J=+1 selection rule, these levels produce absorption lines at 2B(J+1), which are evenly spaced by 2B. This example builds an HCl-like rigid rotor from chemistrykit.quantum, prints its level energies and line positions, checks the constant 2B spacing, and adds the Boltzmann-population intensity pattern seen in a real far-infrared or microwave spectrum.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/spectro/rotational/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_rotational_spectrum_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/spectro/rotational/plot_01_rotational_spectrum`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Dennison's quantized rigid rotor: HCl rotational lines spaced by 2B, with Boltzmann intensities</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/spectro/rotational/plot_01_rotational_spectrum`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Predicts HCl&#x27;s fundamental IR absorption from a harmonic oscillator, compares it against the exact (anharmonic) Morse-potential fundamental and first overtone, then inverts the observed 0\to1/0\to2 band positions to recover the spectroscopic constants \omega_e/\omega_ex_e -- exactly the way real anharmonicity constants are measured from an observed overtone spectrum.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/spectro/vibrational/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_harmonic_vs_morse_thumb.png
